// TEST: Idx Card Witness Cell (rejects)
// ============================================================================
// A CONJUNCT THAT DOES NOT FOLD NAMES THE CELL IT FAILED AT.
//
//   conjunct 1 of Witness did not fold: Static evaluation: undefined variable
//   'not_a_static' at (p = 0, q = 0). Every conjunct is folded once per box
//   cell under a 100000-deep budget, so a conjunct that recurses without a
//   static bound fails at the FIRST cell it is reached with
//
// Two things are being pinned, and they belong to different layers.
//
// THE REASON is the fence's: it folds one cell's conjuncts and reports what
// went wrong with them. THE CELL is the counting layer's, because the fence
// evaluates cells one at a time and has no idea which of the box's cells it
// is on. A per-cell failure without its coordinates is unactionable — the
// user is told that something in a 25-cell box did not fold and left to find
// it — so the enumerator appends the assignment it was holding when the fold
// came back an error.
//
// The cell reported is the FIRST FAILING CELL IN LEX ORDER, which is a real
// commitment and not an accident of iteration: the flat-filter route walks
// the box in lex order and stops at the first error, so the coordinates are
// reproducible rather than whichever cell a scheduler happened to reach.
//
// WHY THE PROBE IS AN UNDEFINED NAME AND NOT THE PLAN'S FUEL BOMB (§6 risk 3).
// `StaticEval.maxSteps` is threaded as `fuel - 1` into every CHILD of a node,
// so it bounds evaluation DEPTH, not step count — and 100,000 nested frames
// of `evalExpr` overflow the .NET stack long before the counter reaches zero.
// A static function that recurses without a static bound therefore kills the
// compiler with an uncatchable StackOverflowException instead of producing
// this diagnostic, and it does so anywhere `let static` folds — a two-line
// program with no struct and no `idx_card` reproduces it. That is a
// pre-existing static-evaluator property that the counting layer neither
// introduces nor can guard against in-process; pinning a live fuel bomb here
// would pin a crash. The witness-cell requirement, which is the part C1 owns,
// is pinned on a conjunct that fails and returns.
//
// SCOPE OF THIS FILE, so it is not read as claiming more than it does: the
// conjunct below fails at EVERY cell, so the reported witness is the origin
// (p = 0, q = 0) BY CONSTRUCTION. What is pinned here is that the suffix
// EXISTS and names a cell at all — not that the witness is non-trivially
// located. The commitment that the witness is the FIRST failing cell in lex
// order is pinned separately in the F# block (Test_StructIdxSpec.fs), on a
// conjunct that folds for p < 2 and fails past it, where the witness is
// (p = 2, q = 0) and the origin would be the wrong answer. The two halves are
// deliberately split: a single conjunct cannot be both always-failing and
// first-failing-somewhere-interesting.
// ============================================================================
static struct Witness {
    p: Int<min=0, max=4>,
    q: Int<min=0, max=4>
} where p + q == not_a_static

let static CARD = idx_card(Witness)
